Hereby I am declaring my candidacy for the Technical Committee.

My involvement with OpenStack began back in 2014 and I've since become core
reviewer for OpenStack Docs and Kolla.

I use OpenStack quite intensively, and I am very familiar with the ecosystem,
history of the project, and everything else related to it. I actively develop
OSISM, an open source deployment and lifecycle management framework for
OpenStack, Ceph and Kubernetes and a reference implementation of the
Sovereign Cloud Stack, as well as Cobalt Core, an open source,
Kubernetes-native OpenStack distribution initiated by SAP for operating
hosted control planes across multi-cluster topologies. With OSISM we are also
an active member of the Sovereign Cloud Stack community and a member of the
OpenInfra Foundation.

Around this, I build and support additional tooling, for example Northwatch,
a real-time analyzer, visualizer and monitor for OVN, or Dizzy, a
scenario-driven load and consistency tester for OpenStack control planes. I
also try to bring projects like these, which are currently not part of the
OpenInfra ecosystem, closer to it or at least make them better known within
the community.

Beyond that, I am active in the extended board and the Technical Steering
Committee of ALASCA, an associate member of the OpenInfra Foundation, and I
am a member of the Governing Board of the NeoNephos Foundation. Through
these roles I have gained a lot of experience with this kind of governance
and cross-community work, and I can help ensure that OpenStack stays well
connected with these adjacent communities and initiatives.

One focus of my work in the TC would be to improve the operability of
OpenStack. Through my current work on Cobalt Core, this insight is once again
very fresh: the individual services differ significantly when it comes to
deployment and configuration, their integration into logging, metering and
tracing, and topics like machine-to-machine authentication with service
accounts. Making all services fully consistent overnight is certainly not
realistic, but I would like to push for incremental steps in that direction,
for example through additional shared oslo libraries and middleware layers,
to make OpenStack easier to deploy and to operate.

I haven't been active in the TC yet. I think that my many years of experience
in deployment and operation in a wide variety of environments will enable me
to contribute quite well.

